  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nWe are to find a function h: [0,2] -> [0,1] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum overlap integral C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx. \nThe current record is 0.38085, and we aim for a lower bound (upper bound on the minimum). The given starting point has n=45 samples. \nWe use a combination of constrained optimization and numerical techniques. The key innovations are:\n1. Using a high-penalty function to enforce the integral constraint while minimizing the overlap\n2. Implementing a local search with L-BFGS-B solver with bounds\n3. Including fallback mechanisms for the integral constraint and convergence\n\nOur goal is to either:\n1. Improve upon the current record by finding a function with lower C_5, or\n2. Provide a fallback solution that shows promise for further improvement\n\nThe approach is nonconvex but bounded, making numerical optimization feasible on 45 variables with finite budget\n\nMathematical specification\n- Representation: n-point piecewise constant approximation on [0,2]\n- Variables: h = (h0, h1, ..., h_{n-1})\n- Integral constraint: sum(h_i) * dx = 1 (dx=2/n)\n- Objective to minimize: max_k (dx * correlation(h, 1-h)[k])\n- Optimizer: L-BFGS-B with finite differences for gradients\n- Penalty function: large penalty added if integral constraint violated\n- Parameters: n=45 (fixed for this run due to time and performance)\n\nImplementation details\n- We use numpy for vectorized operations\n- Total_objective = overlap_value + penalty*(1 - integral)\n- Bounds: 0 <= h_i <= 1 for all i\n- Validation: keep track of best solution meeting integral constraint at every iteration\n- We compute the correlation using np.correlate with mode='full'\n\nSearch choices and variations\n1. We start with the given initial_h_values\n2. Use L-BFGS-B with finite-difference gradients\n3. High penalty for constraint violation to enforce integral condition\n4. Fallback to best_valid if local optimization fails\n\nComputational budget\n- n=45 and the penalty method ensures robustness\n- Owl change implementation for larger n if needed\n\nValidation and return contract\n- Always return 45-point solution\n- Passes integral test, overlap computation, and fallaback\n- Return is (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) with n_points=45",
